Chapter: CatPlanSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Frame
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter DepthSort
  cites Safari chapter CatPlan
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 CatPlan: find the segments that have a cat, clock its crossing from the rider's
 gap, and place the still.

 THE GAP IS THE PART WORTH GRADING AND IT RUNS BACKWARDS. `chain-gap` is how far
 the rider is from the START of chain entry d, so at d = 0 it is MINUS his
 along -- he is past it -- and each further entry adds the length of the one
 BELOW it. Off by one there reads a segment's own length instead of its
 predecessor's, which is the same class of mistake `at`'s prose warns about and
 does not look like corruption: the cat is simply clocked from the wrong place.

 EVERY SEGMENT CARRIES A WELL-FORMED CAT and only some segments have one, which
 World's spec grades from the other side. Here it is the gate that matters: a
 chapter reading the cat without checking `has-cat` would place three cats on a
 three-segment chain and none of them would look wrong on screen.

 THE CAT CULLS ON THE LIVE FOCAL AND AT THE NEAR PLANE, AND NOWHERE ELSE. The
 chapter's prose argues the point -- it is a flat billboard at one depth, so it
 slides off to the left as the rider passes rather than streaking across the
 viewport, and culling it earlier would vanish the collapsed cat exactly as he
 reaches it. Both culls are graded and no third one is.

 We say:

Section: The Chain

 The route's first three segments; segment 1 is the one with a cat.

  segs : List Segment
  segs = [segment-at 0, segment-at 1, segment-at 2]

  chain : List Integer
  chain = [0, 1, 2]

  origin : Pose
  origin = Pose { along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }

  has-got : List Boolean
  has-got = [(list-at segs 0).has-cat, (list-at segs 1).has-cat, (list-at segs 2).has-cat]

  has-want : List Boolean
  has-want = [False, True, False]

Section: The Gap

 Minus the rider's along at the head of the chain, then each entry adds the
 length of the one below it: 500 for the first step and 320 for the second.

  gap-got : List Real
  gap-got =
    [ chain-gap segs chain 0.0 0, chain-gap segs chain 0.0 1, chain-gap segs chain 0.0 2,
      chain-gap segs chain 120.0 0, chain-gap segs chain 120.0 1, chain-gap segs chain 120.0 2 ]

  gap-want : List Real
  gap-want = [0.0, 500.0, 820.0, -120.0, 380.0, 700.0]

Section: The Walk

 One cat on this chain, and it comes from entry 1. The rider rides up segment 0
 toward it; `riding a` puts him a metres along and clocks him from there, so the
 pose and the gap stay consistent.

  riding : Real -> List CatItem
  riding (a) =
    walk-cats segs chain (Pose { along = a, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }) 500.0 a 1.0 0

  seen : List CatItem
  seen = riding 300.0

  count-got : List Integer
  count-got =
    [ list-length seen,
      list-length (seg-cat segs chain origin 0 500.0 0.0 1.0),
      list-length (seg-cat segs chain origin 2 500.0 0.0 1.0),
      max-vis-cats ]

  count-want : List Integer
  count-want = [1, 0, 0, 8]

 It is the chapter's own height, and the still is the resting pose because the
 crossing has not started.

  place-got : List Real
  place-got = [(list-at seen 0).height, (list-at seen 0).lift]

  place-want : List Real
  place-want = [1.7, 0.0]

  pose-got : List Integer
  pose-got = [(list-at seen 0).pose-idx]

  pose-want : List Integer
  pose-want = [0]

 THE CAT IS FIXED AND THE RIDER MOVES, so with no yaw two things follow that are
 worth more than any single coordinate. Its depth falls one metre per metre he
 rides -- the sine of his yaw is exactly zero, so `forward` is the along
 difference alone -- and its `right` does not move AT ALL, for the same reason.
 A composition that leaked the rider's along into the across would still put the
 cat somewhere plausible on every frame.

  closing-got : List Real
  closing-got =
    let a = (list-at (riding 200.0) 0).fwd
    in let b = (list-at (riding 300.0) 0).fwd
    in let c = (list-at (riding 400.0) 0).fwd
    in [b - a, c - b, (list-at (riding 300.0) 0).right - (list-at (riding 200.0) 0).right]

  closing-want : List Real
  closing-want = [-100.0, -100.0, 0.0]

 THE TWO CULLS SIT AT OPPOSITE ENDS OF THE SAME RIDE. From the start of the route
 the cat is over four hundred metres off and under two pixels; a hundred metres
 past it, it is behind the near plane. In between it is drawn, which is what makes
 these two refusals and not one.

  cull-got : List Integer
  cull-got =
    [ list-length (riding 0.0),
      list-length (riding 200.0),
      list-length (riding 500.0),
      list-length (riding 600.0),
      list-length (walk-cats segs [] origin 500.0 0.0 1.0 0),
      list-length (walk-cats segs [0] origin 500.0 0.0 1.0 0) ]

  cull-want : List Integer
  cull-want = [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0]

Section: Into the Frame

  items-got : List Integer
  items-got =
    [ list-length (cat-items seen 0),
      list-length (cat-items [] 0),
      (list-at (cat-items seen 0) 0).i ]

  items-want : List Integer
  items-want = [1, 0, 0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "cp-has   " has-got has-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cp-gap   " gap-got gap-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cp-count " count-got count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cp-place " place-got place-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cp-pose  " pose-got pose-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cp-close " closing-got closing-want 0.0001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cp-cull  " cull-got cull-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cp-items " items-got items-want)
  end
